#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2f03be7e-0a77-5869-b591-6a8f8f3942de" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5814" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the equation of the polynomial above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/d979178b10acbe0ddfed255dad253c61.png)\n\n1. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n2. $y=(x+2)^3(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n3. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2$, $x=-1$, and $x=2$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x =-2$, this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x =-1$, this root has even multiplicity.\n- Since the graph passes through $x = 2$, this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2$, the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is\n\n$$\ny= (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128898 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03ac24f-82a6-5af8-b1b8-9021e521d47a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e158501f-0bac-5d16-956a-59da7f82620f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5818" :question/problem "Which of the above could be the graph of $y = (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/4ca45f72e9238f127f4f2fb3f6072708.png)" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=\\pm 2$ is $1$, which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2$, which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\textbf{I}$ and $\\textbf{III}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, $y$ approaches $\\infty$.\n\nOnly graph $\\textbf{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is **$\\textbf{III}$**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "The graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/9cfc8c5b4d8ae817c96c52ab32266bc9.png)\n\n1. $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n2. $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n3. $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty$." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty$, the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "53aa15a0-8633-51b8-8731-9099eec94940" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9941" :question/problem "A part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/ead16b30a111439d43b0f0b8024f158c.png)\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity\n- $x=-4$ is a simple root" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$<br><br>(Simple Root) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/8d4fa6772f455694bd3de3902a20aeeb.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/70231eae4f8dd6d1843bad449fc2e7c3.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple Root)<br><br>Even | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/858c9a8ff00003e7465a1ff3ed7c096b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/53e2a4b825235fd256356c80c61c67cd.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple Root)<br><br>Odd | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/3d1237ff3984c61c337993fffbfe9a3b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/b341a19379dc575c8b225b2b99d79689.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a$, we have a *simple* root $x=a$.\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a$, we have a *multiple* root $x=a$. Furthermore:If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a$, the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*. If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a$, the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-4$. So, $x=-4$ is a *multiple* root.\n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-4$. So, $x=-4$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30216577-76e8-558c-b2e8-d8a6e5785df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244304} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdc5fea-b91e-521e-9837-4b86b9defed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244305} {:answer/id #uuid "909edf30-af3a-5009-ab35-d81d7ad0fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244306} {:answer/id #uuid "7670b3a6-e4e6-5019-a636-bae969fe728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244307} {:answer/id #uuid "444816b0-ca8d-5e6a-8838-18877dbbfa59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30216577-76e8-558c-b2e8-d8a6e5785df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244304} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca74bbcd-d88b-5d73-8d1a-a188a1e6bf5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb9bcd3c-d3ff-52c3-b152-efe91ebd156b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-112585" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/5e4e2241490626139e15800f3b90c8e15a9963bdcace9907f8557b0897d533e6.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(0)$ is positive.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0,$ which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d4c130-8b5a-5e05-a48f-6d4bea3a5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}x(x-3)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244311} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6048d7-b982-53a8-975e-716c88a2f3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-8{x}^{4}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244312} {:answer/id #uuid "49b52793-e35b-5508-a471-d141859af4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x(x+3)(x-2{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244313} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f89a2-5e94-570b-9a18-c7dd0f9c4ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{4}(x-3{)}^{3}(x-2{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244314} {:answer/id #uuid "30e7684e-ed73-54c7-b84f-f37ab5d39d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30e7684e-ed73-54c7-b84f-f37ab5d39d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244315} :answer-field/id #uuid "35baf619-5f20-5233-affa-5a93ab407e0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e12ebcfe-8743-5040-9b0a-16591b625086" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-112595" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/a5f078132f264a73a4cf2cd1c6f43c61e7f396fa0d6fc8daf2f979c3665ed83e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=0,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=2,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdc1492-a9ae-562a-b521-c0b305248e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244318} {:answer/id #uuid "ad46d746-7648-53a7-910c-7a7a8003261d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{3}(x+2{)}^{2}(x-3{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244319} {:answer/id #uuid "d16c0065-070a-5431-93eb-4c32cb6341cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x(x+2)(x+3{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244320} {:answer/id #uuid "288a0288-d8ec-5858-a4f1-555cef729f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}x(x+2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244321} {:answer/id #uuid "366403ac-7e1e-57d2-8139-b6563fe3ed21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+2{)}^{3}(x+3{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdc1492-a9ae-562a-b521-c0b305248e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244318} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5966a9-00ad-5d27-a4a5-c8dada4b910f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b9ef6a9-d053-5b03-b082-12e556b7bb89" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-112596" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/849535b983a64c97b4cf06afcc7da7598349dc3f14f771cd3ae20db2c0742e8b.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f10636c-d501-5eb0-9e51-46bc6e1430e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244325} {:answer/id #uuid "902da21d-f90a-54f3-b5d8-214a0aa4b336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244326} {:answer/id #uuid "313267f6-a52b-5ab1-8f39-adc12038b6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244327} {:answer/id #uuid "af65ca7e-728d-59d1-8b95-63128b4513e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244328} {:answer/id #uuid "10067495-6d79-5623-acb4-583cd9cdacc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "902da21d-f90a-54f3-b5d8-214a0aa4b336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244326} :answer-field/id #uuid "b74d81c4-5ae4-5ce8-b92e-f7728a632b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244324}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fd7fdcf-ee89-52e6-b0ff-f563716e91a3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-141133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/33bec46293f976bfb829e987cd3ce26fa5cc67310bc830fc8f46b7f0d57b3acf.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ has *odd* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca888811-bbd7-50a0-9f2a-ef0739ee164f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244332} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9bfa58-8626-5e67-a778-76b11ba789ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244333} {:answer/id #uuid "80b15ed9-76b4-5233-a1a0-76ef81f7a42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244334} {:answer/id #uuid "df754432-c054-5f57-9606-40aa7eddecf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244335} {:answer/id #uuid "25593c0f-f34b-5c98-ba04-af9b30576587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b15ed9-76b4-5233-a1a0-76ef81f7a42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244334} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad3729c-f0cc-5bdc-a57d-f6f80541795d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7feb86d4-4bd8-51cc-aea0-45bfacb578de" :question/is-example false 